     "notes": {
      "text": "The opening is a race to develop and control the centre. 1.d4 frees your queen's bishop, stops ...e5 and controls c5. The pawn is already guarded by the queen, so play is usually slower and more positional than after 1.e4, and White often follows with c4 for more control of the centre. 1.e4 is the more popular, sharper choice.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory",
       "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
       "licenceUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
       "authors": "Wikibooks contributors (see the page history)"
      }
     }
